WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.120
Makeshift Flash suits, Kryptonian callbacks, and&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne still getting nuts. Here's a breakdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.320
of some of the teases and Easter eggs that may&nbsp;
have flashed by you in the latest Flash trailer.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.900
The trailer's opening moments take viewers back to&nbsp;
Wayne Manor, but it looks quite different from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.760
building depicted in Tim Burton's Batman movies.&nbsp;
While the manor's exterior is slightly obscured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.080
by mist, this one seems to be much grander than&nbsp;
the one we've seen before. Why could this be?

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.820
Well, Burton's first movie used the iconic&nbsp;
Knebworth House to film the exteriors of Wayne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.400
Manor, and The Flash seems to have followed suit,&nbsp;
as filming took place there in 2021. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.340
it's been over three decades since&nbsp;
the release of Burton's Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.280
so it's quite possible that the&nbsp;
building has had some work done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.180
between then and 2021. In-universe,&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne could have done the same.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.960
In 1989's Batman, Wayne Manor&nbsp;
is mainly shown when the guests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.680
enter the car park in a wide array of&nbsp;
fancy vehicles. The Flash, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.540
sees Bruce and Barry walk down a long footpath&nbsp;
toward the front doors; it's entirely possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.140
that we're seeing a side of the building&nbsp;
that simply hasn't been shown until now.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.620
Helpfully, the trailer for The&nbsp;
Flash reminds us that Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.780
parents are dead — just in case you didn't know.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.500
However, this flick features two older&nbsp;
and wiser iterations of Bruce Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.180
both of whom have had decades to process&nbsp;
their grief. These brutal life experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.560
make both Batmen the perfect father figures&nbsp;
for Barry Allen, another hero who understands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.040
the grief of losing his parents. In their&nbsp;
scenes with the Flash, the Dark Knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.060
reveal that pain has informed their entire&nbsp;
beings. Michael Keaton's Batman tells Flash:

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.740
"That pain made me who I am."

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.380
Ben Affleck's version, meanwhile, informs Allen:

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.880
"These scars we have make us who we are."

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.960
It's not all doom and gloom, though, as&nbsp;
Batfleck also encourages the young hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.260
to not let that pain define him. The&nbsp;
trailer suggests that the Batmen will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.160
have mentor roles in The Flash. And&nbsp;
who knows? Maybe other iterations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.740
the Dark Knight will turn up to&nbsp;
share their own words of wisdom.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.140
The Flash stars not one but two Barry Allens.&nbsp;
To make the experience easier to digest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.560
both Flashes have visually distinct&nbsp;
costumes. In the latest trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.240
Flash Number One is sporting a comics-accurate&nbsp;
red-and-gold super-onesie with lightning bolt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.500
embellishment, and Flash Number Two is&nbsp;
sporting... something else. What is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.140
exactly? Homemade body armor? It's weirdly clunky&nbsp;
for a speedster. Now, this new costume is barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.400
on screen for more than a second, and it's&nbsp;
never seen up close, so it's hard to say for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.080
sure — but it sure looks like Flash Number Two is&nbsp;
strapped inside of a repurposed Batman costume.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.880
Look at it again. You'll notice the red coloring&nbsp;
appears to be a slap-dash paint job with streaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.200
of black peeking through, and that lightning bolt&nbsp;
insignia is definitely painted over a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.540
logo. But the clincher here is the hilariously&nbsp;
thick cowl, a staple feature of Michael Keaton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.440
original Batman design. It looks like the&nbsp;
second Barry "borrowed" Batman's suit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.980
spruced it up at the nearest Michaels. Which&nbsp;
is actually pretty on-brand for the Flash.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.320
While The Flash will undoubtedly have its fair&nbsp;
share of surprises, DC dropped a few cryptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.060
teases in the latest trailer. One interesting&nbsp;
moment shows a mysterious figure standing behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.980
the child as Zod's Kryptonian terraforming device&nbsp;
begins its destruction. The blink-and-you-miss-it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.780
character comes on screen just as we see Barry&nbsp;
running in the city, so those not paying close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.860
attention may assume it's him standing&nbsp;
behind the kid, but it's not... or is it?

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.060
The mystery character looks like they threw&nbsp;
together their outfit at the last second, sporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.800
what appear to be random pieces of orange, black,&nbsp;
and white armor. Unlike the rest of the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.060
in the shot, the character isn't running away&nbsp;
from the destruction, so it definitely gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.440
off the vibe that they're some sort of hero. The&nbsp;
most realistic candidate would be the other Barry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.160
who features throughout the trailer; this&nbsp;
scene might simply take place before he gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.960
his final outfit. However, Flashpoint, the comic&nbsp;
on which The Flash is based, features countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.820
DC characters, so technically, this could be&nbsp;
anyone. It's certainly no innocent bystander.

00:03:42.684 --> 00:03:43.860
As the Joker once asked:

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.560
"Where does he get those wonderful toys?"

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.000
Of course, one of the toys that the Caped&nbsp;
Crusader has at his disposal in that movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.620
his jet fighter, aptly named the Batwing.&nbsp;
This highly stylized aircraft is placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.060
front and center in this new trailer for The&nbsp;
Flash, which is not a huge surprise — since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.260
Batman is just a rich guy without any real&nbsp;
superpowers, he needs some way to get around.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.240
Indeed, the Batwing looks to be an integral part&nbsp;
of The Flash. You may remember the vehicle from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.800
a number of Batman movies, but perhaps its&nbsp;
most iconic screen moment sees Batman scoop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.900
up the Joker's poisonous parade balloons during&nbsp;
the climax of Burton and Keaton's 1989 Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.580
movie. Of course, the Joker isn't the biggest&nbsp;
fan, and he quickly brings down the Batwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.660
with a comically oversized handgun. Whether&nbsp;
or not the Batwing featured in The Flash is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.560
that version or a new one, it's nice to see&nbsp;
that Keaton's Batman still has his wings.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.780
Those who have read DC Comics' Flashpoint will be&nbsp;
all too familiar with what's to come in The Flash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.620
and it certainly looks like audiences&nbsp;
will see Barry Allen and Bruce Wayne's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.660
infamous lightning experiment on the big screen.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.100
In Flashpoint, Barry wakes up in an alternate&nbsp;
reality where his mother never died and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.020
never became the Flash. He soon realizes this new&nbsp;
world is much worse than the one he left behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.120
so he contacts Thomas Wayne, this reality's&nbsp;
Batman, to help him get his powers back. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.360
two attempt to recreate the lightning&nbsp;
strike that gave Barry his superspeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.140
but the first attempt fails, leaving&nbsp;
Barry covered in horrible burns.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.700
The trailer for The Flash offers fans quick&nbsp;
glimpses of a similar experiment. At one point, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:13.320
see one hand touching another twitching, smoking&nbsp;
hand. Later, we get another glimpse of Barry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.120
strapped into a chair as blue lightning sparks all&nbsp;
around him. The Flash isn't a direct adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.680
of Flashpoint, but it seems safe to say fans&nbsp;
will see Barry fighting to get his powers back.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.900
This new trailer offers plenty of footage&nbsp;
featuring both the original Barry Allen and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.820
alternate-timeline Barry, but it's conspicuously&nbsp;
missing a few important aspects of the Flash's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.860
story. Traditionally, Barry tends to lose a parent&nbsp;
or parents to a villainous speedster — usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.540
Reverse-Flash. So what does this trailer tell us&nbsp;
about the movie's potential secret antagonist?

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.420
Well, this Flash trailer shows&nbsp;
the alternate-timeline Barry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:46.980
far angrier than we've seen him before.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.880
It's very possible, then, that he could&nbsp;
end up at odds with our Barry. Since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.260
footage we've seen so far only features two&nbsp;
speedsters, and since it's pretty unlikely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.220
that the central character will break bad, it's&nbsp;
possible that the other version of Barry will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.780
indeed grow disillusioned with the main Flash,&nbsp;
or perhaps become corrupted by the Speed Force.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.800
Turning Barry Allen into his own worst enemy&nbsp;
would certainly be a novel approach to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.420
Flash's speedster-heavy rogues' gallery, and&nbsp;
making this particular version of Reverse-Flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:17.400
responsible for the death of Barry's parents might&nbsp;
just be one of the biggest blows any superhero has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.200
ever faced. Is The Flash willing to go there, or&nbsp;
are there other, as-yet-unseen forces at play?

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:27.480
During the trailer, Kara Zor-El gives Barry&nbsp;
Allen a pop quiz on Kryptonian symbols.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.160
"You know what this symbol stands for?"

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.040
"It means hope, right?"

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.180
It's a brief scene, but this acts as a huge&nbsp;
callback to Man of Steel. During that movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.600
Superman explains the same thing about&nbsp;
the S-shaped shield on his uniform.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:44.400
"It's not an 'S.' On my world, it means 'hope.'"

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.660
With the weight of saving the world resting on&nbsp;
her shoulders, it looks like Kara will literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.740
carry her family's mantle on her chest. She&nbsp;
will now be the symbol of hope her cousin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:56.460
once was — and since Clark definitely wasn't&nbsp;
the type to give up on the good in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.580
it's likely that this philosophy&nbsp;
will be carried through with Kara.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.800
Fans of DC Comics' Flashpoint event know that&nbsp;
an array of heroes and villains appear in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.920
universe-altering story arc. Superman, Aquaman,&nbsp;
Wonder Woman, and Cyborg all play pivotal roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.660
in the story, so The Flash could be hiding any&nbsp;
number of cameos. While it's clear that Supergirl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.160
is taking Superman's place in the movie, could the&nbsp;
Man of Steel himself somehow make an appearance?

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.820
At one point, the trailer for The Flash&nbsp;
shows Supergirl flying through the clouds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.300
carrying a shirtless man. We never&nbsp;
see the face of the mystery character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.580
but could it possibly be a dead or weakened&nbsp;
Kal-El? In the Flashpoint comics, Flash, Cyborg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:37.380
and Batman rescue a frail Superman, who has been&nbsp;
a prisoner since his arrival on Earth. Could The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.100
Flash loosely adapt this, with Supergirl&nbsp;
finding her cousin in a death-like state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.240
forcing her to fly him up toward the sun?&nbsp;
On the other hand, it could just be Barry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.200
who needs to be closer to the storm to get&nbsp;
struck by lightning. While it seems highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.120
unlikely that Henry Cavill secretly returned for&nbsp;
yet another DC cameo, The Flash is definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.200
keeping some things hidden for its theatrical&nbsp;
release — so anything could be possible.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:03.480
As you can probably tell, this new trailer for&nbsp;
The Flash is absolutely filled to the brim with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.520
callbacks and references to previous films&nbsp;
within the DC Multiverse. Michael Keaton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.120
Batman steals the show naturally, and&nbsp;
audiences are treated to costumes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.960
vehicles, and even famous lines from&nbsp;
other movies. Take this, for example:

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:20.760
"You wanna get nuts? Let's get nuts."

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.980
This is actually a reference to a famous line from&nbsp;
1989's Batman film. At one point in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.820
Keaton's Bruce Wayne is at Vicki Vale's&nbsp;
apartment when the Joker bursts in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.700
his gang in tow. When the Joker&nbsp;
attempts to intimidate Bruce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.219
Bruce retorts by flashing a hint of&nbsp;
his own rage and aggressive posture.

00:08:36.219 --> 00:08:39.900
"You wanna get nuts? Come on! Let's get nuts!"

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.240
Hopefully it works out better&nbsp;
for Bruce this time around.

